@page
@model SimulationViewer.Pages.IndexModel

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Super Mendelian Sandbox</title>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/site.css" />
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Super Mendelian Sandbox</h1>

    <p class="lede">
        An agent-based model of genetic control systems in <em>Anopheles</em> mosquitoes.
        Every individual is tracked as a diploid organism with explicit chromosomes,
        loci and alleles, so inheritance, recombination and resistance emerge from the
        genetics rather than being imposed by an equation. Choose a system below to
        configure its parameters, run the simulation and inspect the results.
    </p>

    <div class="model-grid">

        <div class="model-card">
            <div class="model-tag tag-suppress">Suppression</div>
            <h2>Suppressive gene drive targeting a mosquito female fertility gene</h2>
            <p>
                A CRISPR homing drive inserted into a gene females need to reproduce.
                Cas9 and a guide RNA cut the wild-type allele in the germline and
                homology-directed repair copies the construct across, so heterozygotes
                pass the drive to far more than half their offspring. Females left with
                two disrupted copies are sterile, and the accumulating genetic load
                suppresses the population &mdash; unless resistance alleles arise first.
            </p>
            <div class="model-meta">
                Tune Cas9 activity, HDR rate, maternal deposition and target-site conservation.
            </div>
            <a href="/Ffer" class="btn btn-view">Configure &amp; run</a>
        </div>

        <div class="model-card">
            <div class="model-tag tag-suppress">Suppression</div>
            <h2>Driving Y chromosome (X-shredder sex distorter)</h2>
            <p>
                A nuclease carried on the Y chromosome that cleaves the X during male
                meiosis. X-bearing sperm are destroyed, so carrier males sire almost
                only sons and transmit their Y to nearly every offspring. The driving Y
                spreads because it is over-transmitted, and the population collapses as
                females run out &mdash; a mechanism that needs no homing and generates
                no drive-resistant target alleles.
            </p>
            <div class="model-meta">
                Tune X-shredding efficiency and the fertility cost paid by drive males.
            </div>
            <a href="/YDrive" class="btn btn-view">Configure &amp; run</a>
        </div>

        <div class="model-card">
            <div class="model-tag tag-modify">Modification</div>
            <h2>MEDEA selfish genetic element</h2>
            <p>
                Maternal-Effect Dominant Embryonic Arrest: a two-part construct pairing
                a maternally expressed toxin with a zygotically expressed rescue.
                Carrier mothers poison every egg they lay, and only embryos that inherit
                the rescue survive. Non-carriers are removed rather than the population,
                so the element sweeps to fixation while population size stays intact
                &mdash; the basis for driving a cargo gene, such as refractoriness to
                malaria, through a wild population.
            </p>
            <div class="model-meta">
                Tune toxin penetrance, rescue efficiency and the map distance separating them.
            </div>
            <a href="/Medea" class="btn btn-view">Configure &amp; run</a>
        </div>

    </div>

    <div class="info-panel">
        <h2>What all three models share</h2>
        <p>
            Whichever system you pick, the underlying simulation is the same. Five
            populations of 500 mosquitoes sit in a linear chain connected by migration
            that weakens tenfold at each step, so a release into Population 1 has to
            work its way outwards. Generations are discrete and non-overlapping: every
            adult mates, lays eggs and dies, and density-dependent regulation then culls
            the egg pool back to the carrying capacity. Transgenic males are released
            into Population 1 at generation 3, and each run is replicated three times
            from an independent wild-type starting population so you can see how much of
            the outcome is stochastic.
        </p>
        <p>
            The results viewer shows, for each population and each replicate, how allele
            frequencies at the relevant loci change over time alongside adult numbers,
            the sex ratio and the number of viable eggs &mdash; the four readouts that
            together distinguish a drive that is spreading from one that is suppressing,
            stalling or being outrun by resistance.
        </p>
    </div>

</body>
</html>
